I made both fixes and committed them as 4e13f65d. All checks pass: cargo build, cargo test with 0 failures, clippy with -D warnings (I forced a re-lint because the first run came back from cache in 0.07s), cargo fmt -- --check, and tests/harness_logic.sh at 336 passed, 0 failed.

1. CI flake (`tests/harness_logic.sh`). The "budget: numeric honored" check expected exactly 500. The budget calculation reads the clock twice, so if a second ticks over between the two reads it returns 499, which is what turned CI run 42736547831 red. The check now accepts 499 or 500, like the three similar checks below it, with a comment citing the 2026-11-10 CI failure.

2. Testing my copy against yoagent's real code (`src/bash_output_cap.rs`). The old test marker_text_is_yoagents compared my code against a string I had typed myself, so its name claimed a match with yoagent that it never checked. I replaced it with head_tail_and_marker_match_yoagent_bash_tool:
• It runs seq 1 2000 through yoagent 0.27.7's public BashTool with small output caps (100, 101 and 257 bytes).
• It strips the Exit code: 0\n line yoagent puts first, then requires the rest to match HeadTailCap byte for byte on the same output.
• It uses ASCII-only output. With multi-byte characters the two can legitimately differ, because yoagent cuts at a byte and mine moves the cut to a character boundary.

Result: they agree. Same cut points, same omitted-byte count, same marker line. So no upstream issue was needed, and the module doc's "CAN drift" sentence now names the test that pins the copy.

Checking the new test can fail: I changed my marker wording ("omitted" → "dropped") and confirmed the edit had actually applied before running. The test went red; after restoring the code it went green, and the temporary marker was gone.

I also updated ARCHITECTURE.md, which still said the old test "pins the literal, not yoagent's live behaviour". That entry now describes the new test, and the old sentence is kept there marked as superseded.
